# Bidirectional Mirror: network_operations (12 dual pairs expected)
#============================================================================

## SECTION 1: SYMBOLS & NOTATION
##
## All MULTIUSER operations use these primitive symbols:
##
##   ⊙ = operation marker (thing system does for users)
##   : = operation classifier (what tier/type)
##   → = flow/transformation
##   ✓ = verification/success
##   ✗ = failure/abort
##   τ = time reference (timestamp)
##   μ = metric reference (user, session, permissions)
##   λ = lambda/parameter reference
##   ≡ = equivalence/equality check
##   ∋ = contains/membership
##   ⊂ = subset/permission scope
##

---

## SECTION 2: THE PRIMITIVE
##
## One binary choice underlies all multiuser operations:
##
##   SINGLE-USER STATE vs MULTI-USER SHARED STATE
##
## Every operation either:
##   (A) Operates on single user's session in isolation, OR
##   (B) Synchronizes/replicates/conflicts across multiple users
##
## This choice determines the election outcome.

---

## SECTION 3: TIER 1 OPERATIONS — USER IDENTITY & SESSION (<1ms, cached)
##
## Fast cached operations. No ledger creation. State verification only.

⊙:register_user
  Input: user_id, user_name, user_type (user|system|bot)
  Output: session_token, user_record ✓|✗
  Effect: Verify user exists in ledger_users.jsonl OR create new entry
  Time: <1ms (cache hit) | <5ms (first registration)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1, cached)
  Reverse Causality: user_id must be unique BEFORE registration attempt
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches user creation pattern in existing ledger
    2. Clarity: user_id uniquely identifies one person
    3. Visibility: Registration recorded in audit trail
    4. Kindness: User gains explicit identity in system
    5. Scaling: Linear to 1M users, O(1) per registration

⊙:authenticate_user
  Input: user_id, password_hash (or token)
  Output: session_token τ, expiry τ, authenticated ✓|✗
  Effect: Verify credentials match ledger_users entry
  Time: <1ms (cache) | <10ms (full auth check)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1, cached)
  Reverse Causality: Authentication rule exists in ledger BEFORE user tries to auth
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches OS-level authentication pattern
    2. Clarity: One user = one identity = one active session at a time (or many, configured)
    3. Visibility: Auth attempt logged to ledger_authentication_audit.jsonl
    4. Kindness: User can prove identity without exposing password
    5. Scaling: Linear to 1M concurrent users

⊙:create_session
  Input: user_id, auth_token, device_id (optional)
  Output: session_id τ, session_data {user_id, created_at τ, expires_at τ, device_id}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Create new session entry for user. One session per user per device OR multiple sessions allowed (spec'd separately)
  Time: <1ms (cache)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1, cached)
  Reverse Causality: Session structure defined in ledger BEFORE session is created
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches web session pattern (token → context → TTL)
    2. Clarity: One session_id uniquely identifies one user context
    3. Visibility: Session lifecycle visible in ledger_sessions.jsonl
    4. Kindness: User can have multiple devices active simultaneously (design choice)
    5. Scaling: O(1) per session creation, millions of sessions supported

⊙:validate_session
  Input: session_id, current_time τ
  Output: valid ✓|✗, user_id, permissions []
  Effect: Check session token exists, not expired, user still active
  Time: <1ms (in-memory session cache)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1, cached)
  Reverse Causality: Session validation rules exist in spec BEFORE token is presented
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Standard OAuth/JWT validation pattern
    2. Clarity: Invalid session = no access (clear boolean)
    3. Visibility: All access checks logged to ledger_access_log.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Clear error message on expired session
    5. Scaling: O(1) per validation, millions of concurrent sessions

⊙:list_users
  Input: filter {active: bool, type: str, limit: int}
  Output: users [] {id, name, type, created_at τ, active bool}, count int
  Effect: Return matching user records from ledger_users.jsonl
  Time: <10ms (linear scan with filter)
  Ledger: None (TIER 1, read-only)
  Reverse Causality: User filter definition exists BEFORE query
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Standard query pattern (SELECT with WHERE)
    2. Clarity: Filter conditions are explicit (active, type, limit)
    3. Visibility: Query logged to ledger_query_audit.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Can filter to find collaborators easily
    5. Scaling: O(n) linear, acceptable for <1M users

---

## SECTION 4: TIER 2 OPERATIONS — PERMISSIONS & SHARING (<10ms, cached decision)
##
## Cached decision operations. Verify permissions, check ownership, resolve access.

⊙:grant_permission
  Input: grantor_id, grantee_id, resource_id, permission_level (view|edit|admin), scope (resource|subsection|world)
  Output: permission_record {grantor_id, grantee_id, resource_id, permission_level, scope, granted_at τ}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Verify grantor has admin permission on resource, then grant to grantee
  Time: <10ms (permission cache lookup + grant check)
  Ledger: None (TIER 2, cached decision)
  Reverse Causality: Permission hierarchy exists in ledger BEFORE grant is attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches RBAC (role-based access control) pattern
    2. Clarity: Permission levels are exclusive (view⊂edit⊂admin)
    3. Visibility: All grants logged to ledger_permission_audit.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Clear denial reason if grant fails
    5. Scaling: O(1) per grant, millions of permissions supported

⊙:verify_permission
  Input: user_id, action (read|write|delete|admin), resource_id, scope
  Output: permitted ✓|✗, reason str
  Effect: Check if user has required permission on resource
  Time: <5ms (permission cache + role lookup)
  Ledger: None (TIER 2, cached decision)
  Reverse Causality: Permission rules exist in spec BEFORE action is attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Standard authorization check
    2. Clarity: Action either allowed or denied (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Check logged to ledger_access_log.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Clear reason for denial (helps user fix)
    5. Scaling: O(1) per check

⊙:get_user_permissions
  Input: user_id, resource_id (optional filter)
  Output: permissions [] {resource_id, permission_level, scope, granted_by_id, granted_at τ}
  Effect: Return all permissions held by user on all resources OR one specific resource
  Time: <10ms (filter lookup)
  Ledger: None (TIER 2, cached)
  Reverse Causality: Permission structure exists in ledger BEFORE query
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Standard permission enumeration
    2. Clarity: Returns all or filtered permissions clearly
    3. Visibility: Query logged
    4. Kindness: User can see what they have access to
    5. Scaling: O(k) where k = number of permissions per user (typically <100)

⊙:revoke_permission
  Input: grantor_id, grantee_id, resource_id, permission_level
  Output: revoked ✓|✗, revoked_at τ
  Effect: Verify grantor has admin on resource, then revoke permission from grantee
  Time: <10ms (permission check + revoke)
  Ledger: None (TIER 2, cached decision)
  Reverse Causality: Permission rules exist BEFORE revocation attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Inverse of grant_permission
    2. Clarity: Permission either revoked or denied (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Revocation logged to ledger_permission_audit.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Grantee notified permission was revoked
    5. Scaling: O(1) per revocation

⊙:resolve_permission_conflict
  Input: user_id, resource_id, conflicting_permissions [] (e.g., [view, edit, admin])
  Output: resolved_permission (highest level), resolution_method str
  Effect: Given conflicting permission grants, return highest priority
  Time: <5ms (permission priority ranking)
  Ledger: None (TIER 2, cached decision)
  Reverse Causality: Permission hierarchy (view⊂edit⊂admin) exists BEFORE conflict
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Standard permission conflict resolution (take max)
    2. Clarity: One permission returned (the highest)
    3. Visibility: Conflict logged for audit
    4. Kindness: Clear which permission wins and why
    5. Scaling: O(k) where k = number of conflicting grants (typically <5)

---

## SECTION 5: TIER 3 OPERATIONS — PRESENCE & SYNCHRONIZATION (<5ms after first call, creates ledgers)
##
## First call creates ledger. Subsequent calls fast. Records all presence/sync events.

⊙:update_user_presence
  Input: user_id, session_id, status (online|away|offline), last_activity τ, location str (optional)
  Output: presence_record {user_id, session_id, status, last_activity τ, updated_at τ}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Update user presence in ledger_presence.jsonl (create on first call)
  Time: <1ms (in-memory) | <5ms (ledger append, first call)
  Ledger: ledger_presence.jsonl (created on first call, append-only)
  Reverse Causality: Presence structure defined in spec BEFORE update attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches presence tracking pattern (online/away/offline)
    2. Clarity: Status is one of three states (enum)
    3. Visibility: All presence changes in ledger_presence.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Other users see who's online
    5. Scaling: O(1) per update, millions of users supported

⊙:get_user_presence
  Input: user_id (optional, if none returns all)
  Output: presence_data {user_id, status, last_activity τ, location} | presence_data[] (if all)
  Effect: Query ledger_presence.jsonl for one user or all users
  Time: <5ms (ledger read)
  Ledger: ledger_presence.jsonl (read-only)
  Reverse Causality: Presence ledger structure exists BEFORE query
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Standard presence query
    2. Clarity: Returns current presence status
    3. Visibility: Query logged
    4. Kindness: Users can see who's available to collaborate
    5. Scaling: O(1) single user, O(n) all users

⊙:broadcast_event
  Input: sender_id, event_type str, event_data {}, target_users [] (empty = broadcast to all)
  Output: broadcast_record {sender_id, event_type, event_data, target_users, sent_at τ}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Send event to target users (or broadcast). Appended to ledger_events.jsonl
  Time: <5ms (ledger append)
  Ledger: ledger_events.jsonl (created on first call, append-only)
  Reverse Causality: Event structure defined BEFORE broadcast attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Publish-subscribe pattern
    2. Clarity: Event is immutable record of what was sent
    3. Visibility: All broadcasts in ledger_events.jsonl
    4. Kindness: All users see events relevant to them
    5. Scaling: O(1) per broadcast, millions supported

⊙:receive_event
  Input: recipient_id, event_filter {event_type: str, since τ}
  Output: events [] {sender_id, event_type, event_data, sent_at τ}
  Effect: Return events from ledger_events.jsonl matching filter
  Time: <5ms (ledger query)
  Ledger: ledger_events.jsonl (read-only)
  Reverse Causality: Event structure exists BEFORE query
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Standard event consumer pattern
    2. Clarity: Returns matching events only
    3. Visibility: Query logged
    4. Kindness: User sees events they care about
    5. Scaling: O(k) where k = matching events

⊙:sync_user_state
  Input: user_id, device_id, local_state_hash str, remote_state_hash str, deltas [] (changes to apply)
  Output: merged_state {}, sync_record {user_id, device_id, local_hash, remote_hash, deltas_applied int, resolved_conflicts int, synced_at τ}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Merge local changes with remote state. Append to ledger_sync_audit.jsonl
  Time: <10ms (merge + hash) | <5ms (subsequent calls, hashes in cache)
  Ledger: ledger_sync_audit.jsonl (created on first call, append-only)
  Reverse Causality: Merge strategy defined BEFORE sync attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches version control merge pattern
    2. Clarity: State merged deterministically (same inputs = same output)
    3. Visibility: All merges logged in ledger_sync_audit.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Conflicts detected early, user can resolve
    5. Scaling: O(d) where d = delta count (typically <100 per sync)

---

## SECTION 6: TIER 4 OPERATIONS — COLLABORATIVE WORKFLOWS (<100ms, composition of lower tiers)
##
## Composite operations combining TIER 1-3 ops for complex multiuser scenarios.

⊙:invite_to_collaboration
  Input: inviter_id, invitee_id, resource_id, permission_level, message str
  Output: invitation_record {inviter_id, invitee_id, resource_id, permission_level, message, created_at τ, status (pending|accepted|declined)}, ✓|✗
  Effect: [verify_permission(inviter→admin on resource)] → [create_session for invitee if needed] → [send broadcast_event] → [record in ledger_collaboration.jsonl]
  Time: <100ms (4 sub-operations in sequence)
  Ledger: ledger_collaboration.jsonl (created on first call, append-only)
  Reverse Causality: Collaboration workflow defined BEFORE invitation sent
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches team invitation pattern (email, Slack invite, etc.)
    2. Clarity: Invitation has clear status (pending/accepted/declined)
    3. Visibility: Full invitation trail in ledger_collaboration.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Invitee gets clear notification with resource and permission level
    5. Scaling: O(1) per invitation, millions of invitations supported

⊙:accept_collaboration_invitation
  Input: invitee_id, invitation_id, session_id
  Output: accepted_record {invitee_id, invitation_id, accepted_at τ}, ✓|✗
  Effect: [validate_session(invitee)] → [grant_permission(inviter→invitee)] → [update status in ledger_collaboration.jsonl] → [broadcast_event(accept_notification)]
  Time: <100ms (4 sub-operations)
  Ledger: ledger_collaboration.jsonl (append only)
  Reverse Causality: Collaboration acceptance workflow defined BEFORE invitee accepts
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches acceptance pattern (Slack accept, GitHub accept, etc.)
    2. Clarity: Status changed from pending to accepted (boolean state)
    3. Visibility: Acceptance logged in ledger_collaboration.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Inviter is notified immediately
    5. Scaling: O(1) per acceptance

⊙:end_collaboration
  Input: user_id, resource_id, other_user_id, reason str (optional)
  Output: ended_record {user_id, resource_id, other_user_id, ended_at τ, reason}, ✓|✗
  Effect: [verify_permission(user→admin on resource OR owns resource)] → [revoke_permission(other_user on resource)] → [send broadcast_event(collaboration_ended)] → [log to ledger_collaboration.jsonl]
  Time: <100ms (4 sub-operations)
  Ledger: ledger_collaboration.jsonl (append only)
  Reverse Causality: End-collaboration rules defined BEFORE end attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches remove/unshare pattern
    2. Clarity: User either has permission to end OR does not (boolean)
    3. Visibility: Ending logged with reason in ledger_collaboration.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Other user is notified they were removed
    5. Scaling: O(1) per removal

⊙:handle_concurrent_edits
  Input: user_id, resource_id, edit_id, timestamp τ, content_before str, content_after str, other_edits [] (concurrent edits from other users)
  Output: resolved_content str, conflict_records [] {user_id, edit_id, resolution_method}, merged_state, ✓|✗
  Effect: [detect conflicts in other_edits] → [apply operational transform OR CRDT merge] → [log to ledger_conflict_resolution.jsonl] → [broadcast resolved state]
  Time: <100ms (merge algorithm + broadcast)
  Ledger: ledger_conflict_resolution.jsonl (created on first call, append-only)
  Reverse Causality: Conflict resolution strategy (OT vs CRDT) defined BEFORE conflict occurs
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches Google Docs / collaborative editor pattern
    2. Clarity: Conflicts are resolved deterministically (same inputs = same output)
    3. Visibility: Every conflict resolution logged with method in ledger_conflict_resolution.jsonl
    4. Kindness: Both users' edits preserved if possible, no silent data loss
    5. Scaling: O(n) where n = number of concurrent edits (typically <10)

---

## SECTION 7: CONSISTENCY & AUDIT
##
## Verification operations to ensure multiuser system integrity.

⊙:verify_ledger_integrity
  Input: ledger_name str
  Output: integrity_report {ledger_name, entry_count int, hash_chain_valid bool, all_entries_parseable bool, all_timestamps_ordered bool, errors [] str}, ✓|✗
  Effect: Scan ledger file for corruption, bad JSON, timestamp ordering, hash chain validity
  Time: <100ms (linear scan of ledger)
  Ledger: None (read-only verification)
  Reverse Causality: Ledger integrity rules exist in spec BEFORE verification attempted
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches blockchain/IPFS content verification
    2. Clarity: Ledger either valid or invalid with specific errors
    3. Visibility: All errors reported, no silent failures
    4. Kindness: Clear description of what's wrong
    5. Scaling: O(n) linear, acceptable for append-only ledgers <1GB

⊙:audit_multiuser_events
  Input: filter {user_id: str, action: str, since τ, until τ, limit int}
  Output: audit_records [] {timestamp τ, user_id, action, resource_id, result str}, count int
  Effect: Query all multiuser ledgers for matching audit trail
  Time: <50ms (filter across multiple ledgers)
  Ledger: None (read-only, multiple ledgers queried)
  Reverse Causality: Audit structure defined BEFORE query
  Five Gates:
    1. Alignment: Matches audit log pattern (who, what, when, where)
    2. Clarity: Events are ordered by timestamp
    3. Visibility: Complete audit trail available
    4. Kindness: User can prove what they did and when
    5. Scaling: O(n) linear scan acceptable for audit

